          %h2#definition Definition
          %p
            A
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+category"} monoidal category
            is
            %strong semicartesian
            if the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/unit+object"} unit
            for the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensor+product"} tensor product
            is a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/terminal+object"} terminal object
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cartesian+monoidal+category"} cartesian monoidal category
            (where moreover the tensor product is required to be the
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cartesian+product"} cartesian product
          %p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formal+duality"}> Dually
            , a monoidal category is
            %strong semicocartesian
            if the unit for the tensor product is an
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/initial+object"} initial object
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cocartesian+monoidal+category"} cocartesian monoidal category
            (where in addition the tensor product is required to be the
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coproduct"} coproduct
          %p
            Many semicartesian monoidal categories are also
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetric+monoidal+category"} symmetric
          %h2#examples Examples
          %p Some examples of semicartesian monoidal categories that are not cartesian include the following.
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                The opposite of the category
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/FinInj"} FinInj
                of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite+sets"} finite sets
                with
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/injections"} injections
                as morphisms with disjoint union as tensor product. This is the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/free"} free
                symmetric semicartesian monoidal category generated by one object.
            %li
              %p Similarly, the opposite of the category of finite, ordered sets with injective order-preserving functions as morphisms and disjoint union of orders as tensor is the free semicartesian monoidal category generated by one object.
            %li
              %p
                The category of
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/nominal+sets"} nominal sets
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Schanuel+topos"} Schanuel topos
                has a semi-cartesian closed structure with separated product, see Chapter 3.4 of Pitts’ monograph Nominal Sets. This is related to the first example as the Schanuel topos is a topos of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sheaves"} sheaves
                over FinInj, and the semicartesian monoidal structure is given by
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Day+convolution"} Day convolution
                of sheaves using the semicartesian monoidal structure of FinInj.
            %li
              %p
                The category of
                = succeed "s" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poisson+manifold"} Poisson manifold
            %li
              %p
                The opposite of the category of
                = succeed "s" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/associative+algebra"} associative algebra
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/base+field"} base field
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi k
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
                with its usual tensor product
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi A
                        %mo ⊗
                        %mi B
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \otimes B
            %li
              %p
                The category of
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/measurable+locales"} measurable locales
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/commutative+von+Neumann+algebras"} commutative von Neumann algebras
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spatial+tensor+product"} spatial tensor product
            %li
              %p
                The category
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cat"} Cat
                with the non-cartesian
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/funny+tensor+product"} funny tensor product
            %li
              %p
                The category of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strict+2-categories"} strict 2-categories
                with the
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gray+tensor+product"} Gray tensor product
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strict+omega-categories"} strict omega-categories
                with the
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Crans-Gray+tensor+product"} Crans-Gray tensor product
            %li
              %p
                The category of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/affine+space"} affine spaces
                made into a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/closed+monoidal+category"} closed monoidal category
                where the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+hom"} internal hom
                has
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi hom
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo ,
                      %mi y
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} hom(x,y)
                being the set of affine linear maps from
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
                to
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi y
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y
            %li
              %p
                The category of
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/convex+space"} convex spaces
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi hom
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo ,
                      %mi y
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} hom(x,y)
                being the set of convex linear maps from
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
                to
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi y
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y
            %li
              %p
                The thin category associated to the linear order
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mn 0
                      %mo ,
                      %mn ∞
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                      %mo ,
                      %mo ≥
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ([0, \infty], \ge)
                of extended nonnegative real numbers with addition as the tensor product and
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+hom"} internal hom
                as truncated subtraction.
            %li
              %p
                If
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi M
                      %mo ,
                      %mo ⊗
                      %mo ,
                      %mi I
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (M, \otimes, I)
                is any monoidal category,
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi I
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I
                being the monoidal unit, the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/slice+category"} slice category
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi M
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                      %mi I
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M/I
                inherits a monoidal product given by
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi X
                      %mover
                        %mo →
                        %mi f
                      %mi I
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ⊗
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi Y
                      %mover
                        %mo →
                        %mi g
                      %mi I
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi X
                      %mo ⊗
                      %mi Y
                      %mover
                        %mo →
                        %mrow
                          %mi f
                          %mo ⊗
                          %mi g
                      %mi I
                      %mo ⊗
                      %mi I
                      %mo ≅
                      %mi I
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X \stackrel{f}{\to} I) \otimes (Y \stackrel{g}{\to} I) = (X \otimes Y \stackrel{f \otimes g}{\to} I \otimes I \cong I)
              %p
                where the isomorphism displayed is the canonical one. This monoidal product is semicartesian. The forgetful functor
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Σ
                      %mo :
                      %mi M
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                      %mi I
                      %mo →
                      %mi M
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma: M/I \to M
                is strong monoidal, and is universal in the sense of exhibiting the fact that semicartesian monoidal functors and strong monoidal functors form a coreflective sub-bicategory of the bicategory of monoidal categories and strong monoidal functors. (Check this.)
          %h2#internal_logic Internal logic
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+logic"} internal logic
            of a (symmetric) semicartesian monoidal category is
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/affine+logic"} affine logic
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+logic"} linear logic
            but permits the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/weakening+rule"} weakening rule
            (and also the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/exchange+rule"} exchange rule
          %h2#properties Properties
          %h3#semicartesian_vs_cartesian Semicartesian vs. cartesian
          %p
            In a semicartesian monoidal category, any tensor product of objects
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \otimes y
            comes equipped with morphisms
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi p
                    %mi x
                  %mo :
                  %mi x
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi y
                  %mo →
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p_x : x \otimes y  \to x
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi p
                    %mi y
                  %mo :
                  %mi x
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi y
                  %mo →
                  %mi y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p_y : x \otimes y \to y
          %p given by
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi y
                  %mover
                    %mo ⟶
                    %mrow
                      %mn 1
                      %mo ⊗
                      %msub
                        %mi e
                        %mi y
                  %mi x
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi I
                  %mover
                    %mo ⟶
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi r
                        %mi x
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \otimes y \stackrel{1 \otimes e_y}{\longrightarrow} x \otimes I \stackrel{r_x}{\longrightarrow} x
          %p and
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi y
                  %mover
                    %mo ⟶
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi e
                        %mi x
                      %mo ⊗
                      %mn 1
                  %mi I
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi y
                  %mover
                    %mo ⟶
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi ℓ
                        %mi y
                  %mi y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \otimes y \stackrel{e_x \otimes 1}{\longrightarrow} I \otimes y \stackrel{\ell_y}{\longrightarrow} y
          %p
            respectively, where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi e
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} e
            stands for the unique morphism to the terminal object and
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi r
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} r
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℓ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \ell
            are the right and left unitors. We can thus ask whether
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi p
                    %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p_x
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi p
                    %mi y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p_y
            make
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \otimes y
            into the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/product"} product
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            is a cartesian monoidal category. (This theorem has been observed by Eilenberg and Kelly (
            %a{:href => "#EilenbergKelly66"}> 1966
            , p.551), but they may not have been the first to note it.)
          %p
            Alternatively, suppose that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi C
                  %mo ,
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mo ,
                  %mi I
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (C, \otimes, I)
            is a
            %strong symmetric
            monoidal category equipped with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+natural+transformations"} monoidal natural transformations
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi e
                    %mi x
                  %mo :
                  %mi x
                  %mo →
                  %mi I
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} e_x : x \to I
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Δ
                    %mi x
                  %mo :
                  %mi x
                  %mo →
                  %mi x
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Delta_x: x \to x \otimes x
            such that
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %mover
                    %mo ⟶
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi Δ
                        %mi x
                  %mi x
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi x
                  %mover
                    %mo ⟶
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi e
                        %mi x
                      %mo ⊗
                      %mn 1
                  %mi I
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi x
                  %mover
                    %mo ⟶
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi ℓ
                        %mi x
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \stackrel{\Delta_x}{\longrightarrow} x \otimes x \stackrel{e_x \otimes 1}{\longrightarrow} I \otimes x \stackrel{\ell_x}{\longrightarrow} x
          %p and
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %mover
                    %mo ⟶
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi Δ
                        %mi x
                  %mi x
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi x
                  %mover
                    %mo ⟶
                    %mrow
                      %mn 1
                      %mo ⊗
                      %msub
                        %mi e
                        %mi x
                  %mi x
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi I
                  %mover
                    %mo ⟶
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi r
                        %mi x
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \stackrel{\Delta_x}{\longrightarrow} x \otimes x \stackrel{1 \otimes e_x}{\longrightarrow} x \otimes I \stackrel{r_x}{\longrightarrow} x
          %p
            are identity morphisms. Then
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi C
                  %mo ,
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mo ,
                  %mi I
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (C, \otimes, I)
            is a cartesian monoidal category (see
            %a{:href => "#dremodaris"} this MO question
            for discussion of one of the technical details).
          %p
            So, suppose
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi C
                  %mo ,
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (C, \otimes, 1)
            is a semicartesian symmetric monoidal category. The unique map
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi e
                    %mi x
                  %mo :
                  %mi x
                  %mo →
                  %mi I
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} e_x : x \to I
            is a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+natural+transformation"} monoidal natural transformation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Δ
                    %mi x
                  %mo :
                  %mi x
                  %mo →
                  %mi x
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Delta_x: x \to x \otimes x
            obeying the above two conditions,
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi C
                  %mo ,
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (C, \otimes, 1)
            is cartesian. The converse is also true.
          %p
            The characterization of cartesian monoidal categories in terms of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi e
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} e
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Δ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Delta
            is mentioned on page 47 of the slides at:
          %ul
            %li
              John Baez,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Universal algebra and diagrammatic reasoning
              %a{:href => "http://math.ucr.edu/home/baez/universal/"} link
          %p
            However, the characterisation was certainly known earlier: for instance, it appears in the thesis of Kegelmann, in which it is called
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/folklore"} folklore
          %h3#definition_in_terms_of_projections Definition in terms of projections
          %p
            For a monoidal category to be semicartesian it suffices that it admit a family of “projection morphisms”. Specifically, suppose
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            is a monoidal category together with natural “projection” transformations
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msubsup
                    %mi π
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                      %mo ,
                      %mi Y
                    %mn 1
                  %mo :
                  %mi X
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi Y
                  %mo →
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi^1_{X,Y}:X\otimes Y \to X
            such that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msubsup
                    %mi π
                    %mrow
                      %mi I
                      %mo ,
                      %mi I
                    %mn 1
                  %mo :
                  %mi I
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi I
                  %mo →
                  %mi I
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi^1_{I,I}:I\otimes I\to I
            is the unitor isomorphism. Then the composites
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Y
                  %mo ≅
                  %mi I
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi Y
                  %mo →
                  %mi I
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y \cong I\otimes Y \to I
            form a cone under the identity functor with vertex
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi I
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I
            whose component at
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi I
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I
            is the identity; hence
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi I
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/terminal+object"} terminal object
            and so
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            is semicartesian.
          %p
            However, it doesn’t follow from this that the given projections
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msubsup
                    %mi π
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                      %mo ,
                      %mi Y
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi^1_{X,Y}
            are the same as those derivable from semicartesianness! For that one needs extra axioms; see
            %a{:href => "https://golem.ph.utexas.edu/category/2016/08/monoidal_categories_with_proje.html#c056710"} this cafe discussion
            for details.
          %h3#colax_functors Colax functors
          %p
            It is well-known that any functor between cartesian monoidal categories is automatically and uniquely
            = succeed ";" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/colax+monoidal+functor"} colax monoidal
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo ×
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mi F
                  %mi x
                  %mo ×
                  %mi F
                  %mi y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F(x\times y) \to F x \times F y
            for the cartesian product. (This also follows from abstract nonsense given that the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-monad"} 2-monad
            for cartesian monoidal categories is
            = succeed ".)" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/colax-idempotent+2-monad"} colax-idempotent
          %h3#semicartesian_operads Semicartesian operads
          %p
            The notion of
            %span.newWikiWord
              semicartesian operad
              %a{:href => "/nlab/new/semicartesian+operad"} ?
            is a type of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generalized+multicategory"} generalized multicategory
            which corresponds to semicartesian monoidal categories in the same way that
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/operads"} operads
            correspond to (perhaps symmetric) monoidal categories and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lawvere+theories"} Lawvere theories
            correspond to cartesian monoidal categories. Applications of semicartesian operads include:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a{:href => "http://golem.ph.utexas.edu/category/2009/10/generalized_operads_in_classic.html"} Generalized operads in classical algebraic topology
                (blog post) – this also uses the above fact about colax functors
            %li
              %p
                %a{:href => "http://golem.ph.utexas.edu/category/2011/05/an_operadic_introduction_to_en.html#c038131"} Characterizing finite measure spaces
                (blog comment)
          %p
            See also
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+of+operators"} category of operators
          %h2#related_pages Related pages
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cartesian+monoidal+category"} cartesian monoidal category
            %li
              %p
                A
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/relevance+monoidal+category"} relevance monoidal category
                is the “dual” of a semicartesian monoidal category, with diagonals but not projections.
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+of+operators"} category of operators
          %h2#reference Reference
          %ul
            %li#EilenbergKelly66
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Samuel+Eilenberg"}> Samuel Eilenberg
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/G.+Max+Kelly"} G. Max Kelly
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Closed Categories
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Samuel+Eilenberg"} S. Eilenberg
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/D.+K.+Harrison"} D. K. Harrison
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/S.+MacLane"} S. MacLane
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/H.+R%C3%B6hrl"} H. Röhrl
                (eds.):
                = succeed "," do
                  %em
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Proceedings+of+the+Conference+on+Categorical+Algebra+-+La+Jolla+1965"} Proceedings of the Conference on Categorical Algebra - La Jolla 1965
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-642-99902-4"} doi:10.1007/978-3-642-99902-4
            %li
              %p
                %span.newWikiWord>
                  Malte Gerhold
                  %a{:href => "/nlab/new/Malte+Gerhold"} ?
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %span.newWikiWord
                    Stephanie Lachs
                    %a{:href => "/nlab/new/Stephanie+Lachs"} ?
                = succeed "," do
                  %span.newWikiWord
                    Michael Schürmann
                    %a{:href => "/nlab/new/Michael+Sch%C3%BCrmann"} ?
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Categorial Lévy Processes
                = succeed "." do
                  %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1612.05139"} arXiv:1612.05139
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Tom+Leinster"}> Tom Leinster
                ,
                %a{:href => "https://golem.ph.utexas.edu/category/2016/08/monoidal_categories_with_proje.html"} Monoidal Categories with Projections
                (blog discussion)
            %li#dremodaris
              %p
                MO user ‘dremodaris’,
                %em A semicartesian monoidal category with diagonals is cartesian: proof?
                %a{:href => "https://mathoverflow.net/q/348480/"} MO348480
